From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 88/91] Skip the invalidate after a wait for ranges completion
 records delivered

After every satisfied fence or semaphore wait cabooseInvalidateCoherentMappings read every
GPU-written range of coherent memory back from the phone, in 64 KiB round trips, whether or
not the application ever read it: in five seconds of Halo 26 calls on DXVK's queue thread
waited 519 ms. With completion payloads (hello 0x200000) the record of the batch that wrote
a range already stored its bytes in the shadow. The tracker now notes, before the batch
completes, which ranges each record stored (caboose-bounded-memory/completion_feed.h
takeDelivered); the invalidate after the wait subtracts them from the batch's writes and
reads only the rest: ranges a record left out for size, wrote for an older batch, a capture
that failed, and every write without the feed or the grant are read as before.

The writes a command buffer, a submission or an unwatched copy marks are kept per
allocation as up to eight disjoint spans instead of one hull
(caboose-bounded-memory/gpu_writes.h), and the invalidate reads each span as its own range
unless reading neighbours as one takes no more round trips; all ranges still go in one call,
which publishes all of them or none. A copy from an image marks the bytes it writes, from the
copied aspect's texel blocks and the region's row length, image height, extent and layers,
instead of 16 bytes per texel of the whole extent. CABOOSE_GPU_MEMORY_TRAFFIC reports the
ranges and bytes delivered (coherent_delivered, coherent_delivered_bytes) beside those read
back (coherent_invalidates, coherent_invalidated_bytes).
